<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National People's Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as NPC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National People's Congress is the national legislature of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> and, under the constitution, the highest organ of state power. First convened in 1954, it has nearly 3,000 deputies elected indirectly for five-year terms and meets once a year; its Standing Committee legislates between sessions. In practice it approves decisions taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress.jpg" alt="National People's Congress" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: multiple people, e.g. Lin Huiyin and Liang Sicheng, see [2] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%AD%E8%8F%AF%E4%BA%BA%E6%B0%91%E5%85%B1%E5%92%8C%E5%9C%8B%E5%9C%8B%E5%BE%BD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Congress traces its origins to the First National Congress of the Chinese Soviets of Workers', Peasants' and Soldiers' Deputies, held on 7 November 1931 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruijin">Ruijin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>, on the anniversary of the Russian October Revolution. A second Soviet Congress followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> on 18 March 1932, and the official Second National Congress met from 22 January to 1 February 1934, when only 693 deputies were elected, 117 of them seats taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-red-army">Chinese Red Army</a>.</p><p>At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> held a Political Consultative Conference, the first Political Consultative Assembly meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> from 10 to 31 January 1946. A second conference in September 1949 approved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-program">Common Program</a>, which served as the de facto constitution for the next five years, and chose the national anthem, flag, capital and state name of the People's Republic. Renamed the People's Political Consultative Conference, it acted as a de facto legislature until the Constitution of 1954 transferred that function to the National People's Congress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The full Congress meets once a year, usually in the spring, for 10 to 14 days in the Great Hall of the People on the west side of Tiananmen Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Its sessions coincide with those of the National Committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-peoples-political-consultative-conference">Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference</a>, and together they are called the Two Sessions. The deputies, elected by provincial-level people's congresses, which are themselves indirectly elected, serve five-year terms; the work is part-time and unpaid. Before each session a Presidium is elected to run the daily business.</p><p>Between sessions the Standing Committee, of roughly 170 members, meets every two months and passes most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s laws. It cannot amend the constitution or appoint or remove national-level personnel. Ten special committees draft and discuss legislation in specific fields. Under the constitution the Congress elects the president, the vice president, the chairperson of the Central Military Commission, and the heads of the Supreme People's Court, the Supreme People's Procuratorate and the National Supervisory Commission. It alone may amend the constitution, by a two-thirds vote of all deputies. Its organic law requires it to uphold the leadership of the Communist Party, and scholars have often called it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> stamp, though laws have occasionally failed to pass there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National People's Congress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The People’s Republic is a party-state: the Chinese Communist Party, through its Politburo and its Standing Committee, directs the government, the army and the mass organisations, while the <b>National People’s Congress</b> serves as the formal legislature.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…anthem was booed, and that night by large crowds in more than a dozen shopping malls at 8:31 pm, a reference to 31 August 2019 and to the decision of the <b>National People's Congress</b> Standing Committee of 31 August 2014; secondary school students sang it in human chains.</blockquote><a href="/songs/glory-to-hong-kong">Glory to Hong Kong</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>National People's Congress</b> restored it on 5 March 1978 with new words naming the Communist Party, communism and Chairman Mao; after Tian's posthumous rehabilitation in 1979 his original words were restored and on 4 December 1982 it became the official national anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a></li><li><blockquote>Its success brought its composer office: Wang Shuangyin became a deputy to the <b>National People's Congress</b>, a member of the Heilongjiang provincial party committee, head of the province's model opera troupe and deputy director of its culture bureau, and was received by Jiang Qing.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li></ul></section>
